259	<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
260	<section>
261	<h2>Part 1 — Multiple Choice (8 questions)</h2>
262	
263	<!-- Q1 -->
264	<div class="question-block" id="q1">
265	  <div class="q-num">Question 1</div>
266	  <div class="q-text">What does the <code>PYBIND11_MODULE(oil_volume_cpp, m)</code> macro expand to under the hood?</div>
267	  <div class="options">
268	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q1',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>A Python class definition that registers the module in <code>sys.modules</code></button>
269	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q1',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>An <code>extern "C"</code> function named <code>PyInit_oil_volume_cpp</code> that Python's import machinery calls when loading the shared library</button>
270	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q1',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>A code-generation step that emits a <code>.py</code> wrapper file alongside the <code>.so</code></button>
271	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q1',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>A linker script that merges the C++ object file with <code>libpython</code></button>
272	  </div>
273	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q1-fb-correct">Correct. The macro emits <code>extern "C" PyObject* PyInit_oil_volume_cpp()</code> — the exact symbol Python's import system looks for when it <code>dlopen()</code>s the shared library. The name must exactly match the module name.</div>
274	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q1-fb-wrong">Not quite. The macro expands to <code>extern "C" PyObject* PyInit_oil_volume_cpp()</code> — an entry-point function Python's loader calls after <code>dlopen()</code>. No code generation, no class, no linker script.</div>
275	</div>
276	
277	<!-- Q2 -->
278	<div class="question-block" id="q2">
279	  <div class="q-num">Question 2</div>
280	  <div class="q-text">Why does <code>bindings.cpp</code> include <code>&lt;pybind11/stl.h&gt;</code> in addition to <code>&lt;pybind11/pybind11.h&gt;</code>?</div>
281	  <div class="options">
282	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>To enable multi-threading support</button>
283	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>To link against the C++ standard library at compile time</button>
284	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>To activate the type casters that convert <code>std::vector&lt;double&gt;</code> to a Python <code>list</code> and vice versa</button>
285	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q2',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>To include the STL allocator so pybind11 can allocate Python objects from a C++ allocator</button>
286	  </div>
287	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q2-fb-correct">Correct. <code>pybind11/stl.h</code> provides type caster specialisations for standard containers: <code>std::vector</code>, <code>std::map</code>, <code>std::set</code>, etc. Without it, the compiler would error: "cannot convert argument of type std::vector".</div>
288	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q2-fb-wrong">No. <code>pybind11/stl.h</code> is about type casters — it tells pybind11 how to convert C++ standard containers to their Python equivalents (and back) at the call boundary.</div>
289	</div>
290	
291	<!-- Q3 -->
292	<div class="question-block" id="q3">
293	  <div class="q-num">Question 3</div>
294	  <div class="q-text">What problem does <code>set(PYBIND11_FINDPYTHON ON)</code> in CMakeLists.txt solve?</div>
295	  <div class="options">
296	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>It switches from static linking to dynamic linking of the pybind11 library</button>
297	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>It makes pybind11 use the modern <code>FindPython3</code> CMake module instead of the deprecated <code>FindPythonLibs</code>, allowing it to correctly find headers in virtual environments and uv-managed Pythons</button>
298	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>It enables Python to find the compiled extension without modifying <code>sys.path</code></button>
299	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q3',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>It disables the default Python discovery so the user must supply <code>-DPython3_EXECUTABLE</code> manually</button>
300	  </div>
301	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q3-fb-correct">Correct. The legacy <code>FindPythonLibs</code> only searches system paths. <code>FindPython3</code> derives header paths from any Python executable you point it at — essential for venv and uv-managed interpreters.</div>
302	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q3-fb-wrong">Not right. The flag tells pybind11 which CMake Python-discovery module to use: the modern <code>FindPython3</code> (which works with venvs and uv Pythons) vs the deprecated <code>FindPythonLibs</code> (which only looks at system paths).</div>
303	</div>
304	
305	<!-- Q4 -->
306	<div class="question-block" id="q4">
307	  <div class="q-num">Question 4</div>
308	  <div class="q-text">What does <code>pybind11_add_module(oil_volume_cpp bindings.cpp)</code> do that a plain <code>add_library(oil_volume_cpp SHARED bindings.cpp)</code> does not?</div>
309	  <div class="options">
310	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>It enables C++17 on the target automatically</button>
311	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>It copies the <code>.so</code> file into <code>site-packages</code> after linking</button>
312	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>It sets the correct Python extension suffix, strips the <code>lib</code> prefix, links Python headers, applies <code>-fvisibility=hidden</code>, and enables LTO in Release mode</button>
313	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q4',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>It runs the pybind11 code generator to produce the <code>PyInit_</code> function</button>
314	  </div>
315	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q4-fb-correct">Correct. That single CMake function handles all the Python-specific build details so you don't have to set them manually.</div>
316	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q4-fb-wrong">No. <code>pybind11_add_module</code> is a smart wrapper around <code>add_library(SHARED)</code> that sets the correct Python extension suffix, strips the lib prefix, applies <code>-fvisibility=hidden</code>, and enables LTO in Release mode.</div>
317	</div>
318	
319	<!-- Q5 -->
320	<div class="question-block" id="q5">
321	  <div class="q-num">Question 5</div>
322	  <div class="q-text">In the hybrid <code>find_package</code> / <code>FetchContent</code> strategy in this repo, what happens when <code>find_package(pybind11 CONFIG QUIET)</code> succeeds?</div>
323	  <div class="options">
324	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',0,true)"><span class="option-key">A</span>CMake uses the pip-installed pybind11 CMake config files and skips the <code>FetchContent_Declare</code> block entirely — no network call is made</button>
325	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>CMake still runs <code>FetchContent_MakeAvailable</code> but uses it only for the version check</button>
326	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>CMake downloads pybind11 from GitHub and overwrites the pip-installed one</button>
327	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q5',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>CMake prefers the FetchContent version if the network is available</button>
328	  </div>
329	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q5-fb-correct">Correct. The <code>if(NOT pybind11_FOUND)</code> guard means the FetchContent block only runs when <code>find_package</code> fails. This makes configure-time fast when pybind11 is pip-installed.</div>
330	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q5-fb-wrong">The <code>if(NOT pybind11_FOUND)</code> guard in CMakeLists.txt skips the <code>FetchContent_Declare</code> entirely when <code>find_package</code> succeeds. No network call is made.</div>
331	</div>
332	
333	<!-- Q6 -->
334	<div class="question-block" id="q6">
335	  <div class="q-num">Question 6</div>
336	  <div class="q-text">After running <code>pip install -e .</code>, you modify <code>bindings.cpp</code> to add a new function. What do you need to do before Python will see the new function?</div>
337	  <div class="options">
338	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q6',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>Nothing — editable installs pick up C++ changes automatically like they do for Python files</button>
339	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q6',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>Restart the Python interpreter — it will recompile on next import</button>
340	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q6',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>Re-run <code>pip install -e .</code> or <code>./scripts/build_cpp.sh</code> to recompile the <code>.so</code>, then restart the interpreter</button>
341	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q6',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>Run <code>cmake --install _build</code> to push the new <code>.so</code> to <code>site-packages</code></button>
342	  </div>
343	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q6-fb-correct">Correct. Editable installs only make Python source changes live. The compiled <code>.so</code> must be rebuilt explicitly. The interpreter also needs to be restarted since Python caches the loaded module object.</div>
344	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q6-fb-wrong">Editable mode only means Python source files are live. C++ changes require a full recompile. Run <code>pip install -e .</code> or the build script, then restart the interpreter (shared libraries are cached in <code>sys.modules</code>).</div>
345	</div>
346	
347	<!-- Q7 -->
348	<div class="question-block" id="q7">
349	  <div class="q-num">Question 7</div>
350	  <div class="q-text">The parity test in <code>test_cpp_bridge.py</code> uses a tolerance of <code>1e-9</code>. Why not <code>0.0</code> (exact equality)?</div>
351	  <div class="options">
352	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>Because the C++ implementation deliberately approximates the result for speed</button>
353	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',1,true)"><span class="option-key">B</span>Because different evaluation orders and FMA instructions in C++ can produce results that differ from NumPy at the level of floating-point rounding, even when both implementations are correct</button>
354	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',2,false)"><span class="option-key">C</span>Because the test converts between Python <code>float</code> and C++ <code>double</code>, introducing a fixed precision loss at the boundary</button>
355	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q7',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>Because NaN values cannot be compared with <code>==</code> in Python</button>
356	  </div>
357	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q7-fb-correct">Correct. Floating-point arithmetic is not associative: <code>(a+b)+c != a+(b+c)</code> in general. Different compilers, optimization flags (especially FMA), and evaluation orders can produce results that differ in the last few ULPs even for mathematically identical algorithms.</div>
358	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q7-fb-wrong">The reason is floating-point non-associativity. Different evaluation orders between C++ (possibly using FMA) and NumPy can cause ULP-level differences even when both algorithms are correct. <code>1e-9</code> is tight enough to catch logic bugs but tolerant of rounding differences.</div>
359	</div>
360	
361	<!-- Q8 -->
362	<div class="question-block" id="q8">
363	  <div class="q-num">Question 8</div>
364	  <div class="q-text">What does the ABI tag in the filename <code>oil_volume_cpp.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so</code> indicate?</div>
365	  <div class="options">
366	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q8',0,false)"><span class="option-key">A</span>The extension was compiled with GCC 3.12 and requires that exact compiler version to load</button>
367	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q8',1,false)"><span class="option-key">B</span>The extension uses the Python Limited API and is compatible with all Python versions from 3.12 onward</button>
368	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q8',2,true)"><span class="option-key">C</span>The extension was compiled for CPython 3.12 on x86-64 Linux and is not binary-compatible with other Python versions, architectures, or implementations</button>
369	    <button class="option-btn" onclick="answer('q8',3,false)"><span class="option-key">D</span>The extension requires GLIBC 3.12 or newer</button>
370	  </div>
371	  <div class="feedback correct" id="q8-fb-correct">Correct. The cpython-312 tag means it was compiled against CPython 3.12's C API. If you upgrade to Python 3.13, you must recompile. The platform tag (x86_64-linux-gnu) means it will not run on ARM or Windows.</div>
372	  <div class="feedback wrong"   id="q8-fb-wrong">The ABI tag encodes the Python implementation (cpython), version (3.12), architecture (x86_64), and OS (linux-gnu). The <code>.so</code> is binary-incompatible with Python 3.11, 3.13, ARM, or PyPy without recompilation.</div>
373	</div>
